Yes. I would make uncertainty a first-class state, not explanatory prose tucked away somewhere.

The application window can explicitly show something like:

APPLICATION WINDOW / OPEN

and beneath it, very small, almost like system telemetry:

* participants / unknown
* production budget / none secured
* jury / unknown
* selection / unknown
* response time / unknown
* exhibition feasibility / conditional
* future contributors / open
* next state / unresolved

That is stronger than pretending the system is already coherent. It says: the project is live because the uncertainties are live.

Conceptually, I would distinguish three classes:

\text{known} \qquad \text{open} \qquad \text{unknown}

For example:

KNOWN
initial contributors
photographic material
submission deadline
production responsibility

OPEN
new participants
future practices
resource contributions
opening format

UNKNOWN
jury composition
selection outcome
whether sufficient resources will emerge
whether the exhibition will actually happen
when or whether Fotografiska responds

The nice thing is that these unknowns are not weaknesses. They are literally part of the state space at t=0.

You could even have one line:

We do not know whether this will happen. That is part of the current state.

That gives the project a lot of credibility. No bluffing, no fake inevitability.

And later, as reality answers some of these questions, the system can visibly update:

UNKNOWN → KNOWN
OPEN → COMMITTED
CONDITIONAL → ACTIVATED

That will make the app feel genuinely temporal rather than merely descriptive.

The one thing I would avoid is making uncertainty sound anxious. Present it clinically, almost instrumentally. Not “will this ever work?”, but:

EXHIBITION / feasibility: unresolved

That keeps the tone sharp.
